window.ECONOMICS_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"nature-concepts","old":{"title":"Nature of economics, basic concepts and economic systems","body":"Economics is the study of how people use scarce resources to satisfy their unlimited wants. Lionel Robbins (1932) defined it as \"the science which studies human behaviour as a relationship between ends and scarce means which have alternative uses\". Adam Smith, whose \"Wealth of Nations\" (1776) earned him the title \"father of economics\", saw it as the study of the wealth of nations.\n\nBasic concepts:\n• Wants: human desires for goods and services; they are unlimited and recurring.\n• Scarcity: resources (land, labour, capital, entrepreneur) are limited relative to wants. Scarcity is the central problem of economics.\n• Choice: because of scarcity we must choose which wants to satisfy.\n• Scale of preference: a list of wants arranged in order of importance, so the most pressing are met first.\n• Opportunity cost: the next best alternative forgone. If a farmer uses his land for cassava instead of yam, the yam he could have grown is the opportunity cost. It is \"real cost\", not a money cost.\n• Free goods (air, sunlight) have no opportunity cost; economic goods are scarce and command a price.\n• Production possibility curve (PPC): shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce with its resources fully used. Points inside = unemployment or waste; outside = unattainable now; outward shift = growth.\n\nThe basic economic problems every society must answer: what to produce, how to produce, for whom to produce, and how efficiently resources are used and whether they will grow.\n\nEconomic systems:\n• Capitalist (free market, laissez-faire): private ownership of resources, the profit motive, the price mechanism (\"invisible hand\") and consumer sovereignty decide what is produced. Merits: efficiency, choice, initiative. Demerits: inequality, monopoly, neglect of public goods.\n• Socialist (command, centrally planned): the state owns the major means of production and a planning authority decides output and prices (the former USSR; North Korea today). Merits: less inequality, social welfare. Demerits: bureaucracy, little incentive, shortages.\n• Mixed economy: private and public sectors coexist, and government corrects market failures. Nigeria is a mixed economy: private firms such as Dangote Group work alongside government agencies and enterprises.\n• Some writers also list the traditional (subsistence) economy.\n\nEconomics as a science: it uses positive statements (what is: \"inflation is 20%\"), which can be tested, and normative statements (what ought to be: \"fuel subsidy should return\"), which are value judgements. Microeconomics studies individual units (a household, a firm, a market); macroeconomics studies the whole economy (national income, inflation, unemployment). \"Ceteris paribus\" means other things being equal.\n\nMarket failure terms JAMB uses: public goods (non-rival, non-excludable), merit goods (underconsumed), demerit goods (overconsumed), externalities (costs or benefits to third parties, e.g. gas flaring).\n\nCommon traps:\n• Opportunity cost is the NEXT BEST alternative only, not all alternatives and not the money price.\n• Scarcity is relative to wants: a rich country still faces scarcity.\n• Consumer sovereignty belongs to the capitalist system, not the socialist one.\n• Micro = small units; macro = aggregates. National income is macro.\n• A point inside the PPC shows unemployed resources, not impossible output."},"new":{"title":"Nature of economics, basic concepts and economic systems","body":"NATURE OF ECONOMICS — THINK LIKE AN ECONOMIST\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nEvery economy faces a problem: people want more than the available resources can provide. Economics studies how individuals, firms and governments make choices under that constraint. JAMB often tests not just definitions, but the difference between closely related concepts.\n\n1. WHAT ECONOMICS STUDIES\nLionel Robbins (1932) defined economics as the study of human behaviour as a relationship between ends and scarce means that have alternative uses. Unpack this:\n• Ends = wants or objectives, such as food, education and healthcare.\n• Scarce means = limited money, time, land, labour and other resources.\n• Alternative uses = the same resource can serve different purposes.\n• Choice = selecting one use means giving up another.\n\nAdam Smith published The Wealth of Nations in 1776 and is conventionally called the father of modern economics. His work emphasised production, trade and national wealth. Robbins's definition instead highlights scarcity and choice.\n\nEXAMPLE: A student has ₦5,000 and needs both a textbook and transport. Buying the textbook may mean walking to school. The problem is not simply poverty: it is that available resources cannot satisfy every want simultaneously.\n\n2. SCARCITY, SHORTAGE AND POVERTY ARE NOT SYNONYMS\n• Scarcity: the universal condition of limited resources relative to wants. It exists even in rich countries.\n• Shortage: quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price; it can be temporary.\n• Poverty: insufficient income or resources to meet a defined standard of living.\n\nA fuel queue caused by a price ceiling or disrupted supply is a shortage. The general need to choose between roads, schools and hospitals reflects scarcity.\n\n3. BASIC ECONOMIC CONCEPTS\n• Wants: desires for goods and services; many recur after satisfaction.\n• Needs: essentials for basic survival and well-being; the boundary may vary by context.\n• Scale of preference: a ranked list of wants according to priority.\n• Choice: selecting among competing alternatives.\n• Opportunity cost: the value of the NEXT BEST alternative forgone, not the sum of everything rejected.\n• Resources/factors of production: land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship.\n• Efficiency: obtaining maximum feasible output from available resources.\n• Equity: fairness in the distribution of economic benefits and burdens.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A local council can build either a clinic, a market or a sports ground. It chooses the clinic; the market was its second choice. The opportunity cost is the market, not the market plus the sports ground.\n\n4. THE THREE CENTRAL ECONOMIC QUESTIONS\nEvery economic system must decide:\n• What to produce? For example, more rice or more industrial machinery?\n• How to produce? Labour-intensive methods or capital-intensive machinery?\n• For whom to produce? Who receives the output and on what basis?\n\nA fourth useful question is how to promote growth and adapt production over time.\n\n5. PRODUCTION POSSIBILITY CURVE (PPC)\nA PPC shows the maximum combinations of TWO goods an economy can produce with given resources and technology, assuming efficient use.\n• On the curve: productively efficient.\n• Inside the curve: feasible but inefficient, such as unemployed labour or idle machines.\n• Outside the curve: unattainable with current capacity.\n• Movement along the curve: more of one good requires less of the other.\n• Outward shift: growth in resources, technology or productivity.\n• Inward shift: loss of productive capacity, for example severe destruction of capital.\n\nWHY IS A TYPICAL PPC BOWED OUT? Resources are not equally suited to every use. As production shifts towards one good, increasing quantities of the other must be sacrificed: increasing opportunity cost. A straight PPC represents constant opportunity cost.\n\nEXAMPLE: If Nigeria reallocates workers and machines from textiles to food production, more food may mean fewer textiles. Improved farming technology can shift the feasible frontier outward.\n\n6. MICROECONOMICS VS MACROECONOMICS\n• Microeconomics studies individual consumers, firms, industries and specific markets: the price of garri, a firm's output, household spending.\n• Macroeconomics studies economy-wide aggregates: inflation, unemployment, GDP, economic growth and national income.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A question about the price of petrol in a specific market is generally microeconomic; a question about the overall inflation rate is macroeconomic.\n\n7. POSITIVE VS NORMATIVE ECONOMICS\n• Positive statement: describes or predicts what is; evidence can test it. Example: “A higher tax on a product may reduce purchases, other things equal.”\n• Normative statement: expresses what ought to be; it involves a value judgement. Example: “Government should make university education free.”\n\nThe word “should” is often a clue, but identify the underlying value judgement rather than relying on one word.\n\n8. ECONOMIC SYSTEMS — WHO MAKES THE DECISIONS?\n• Traditional: custom and inherited practices guide production and distribution.\n• Market/capitalist: private ownership and prices guide many decisions through demand and supply.\n• Command/socialist planned: public authorities centrally direct major production and allocation decisions.\n• Mixed: markets and government both play important roles; most contemporary economies, including Nigeria, combine them.\n\nDO NOT CONFUSE: A mixed economy is not an equal 50–50 split between state and private ownership. It simply combines market mechanisms with public intervention.\n\n9. WHY GOVERNMENTS INTERVENE\nMarkets may underprovide public goods, ignore external costs such as pollution, permit monopoly power or produce unequal outcomes. Governments use taxes, subsidies, regulation and public services. Intervention can also be inefficient, so economists compare costs and benefits.\n\nQUICK COMPARISONS\nScarcity ≠ shortage.\nOpportunity cost ≠ money spent.\nPPC inside ≠ impossible; PPC outside = currently unattainable.\nMicro = individual market; macro = economy-wide.\nPositive = testable claim; normative = value judgement.\nMarket economy ≠ absence of all government.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — ECONOMIC REASONING IN REAL LIFE\n\n10. FACTORS OF PRODUCTION AND THEIR REWARDS\n• Land includes natural resources such as farmland, forests, water and minerals; its conventional reward is rent.\n• Labour means human physical and mental effort; its reward is wages or salaries.\n• Capital means man-made productive assets such as machinery, tools and factory buildings; its reward is interest in the conventional factor-reward classification.\n• Entrepreneurship coordinates production, takes business risks and innovates; its reward is profit.\n\nIMPORTANT DISTINCTION: Money used to purchase a machine is financial capital; the machine itself is physical capital. A bank deposit is not itself a machine or factory. In everyday language “capital” may mean money, but exam questions often mean produced means of production.\n\n11. PRODUCTION, CONSUMPTION AND DISTRIBUTION\nProduction creates utility by making goods or services available in a useful form, place or time. Consumption uses goods and services to satisfy wants. Distribution concerns how output and income are shared among people and factors of production.\n\nA farmer grows cassava (production), a transporter moves it to a market (place utility), a processor makes garri (form utility), and a household eats it (consumption). The activities are linked, not separate economic worlds.\n\n12. ECONOMIC GOODS, FREE GOODS AND PUBLIC GOODS\nAn economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost. A free good, in the textbook sense, is available without scarcity in the relevant setting, such as abundant sunlight. A “free” school meal funded by taxes is NOT a free good economically: resources were used to provide it.\n\nPublic goods are generally non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence. They are not defined by being government-owned or having a zero price. A public hospital is a publicly provided service, but an individual hospital bed is rival in consumption.\n\n13. THE PPC AS A STORY ABOUT TRADE-OFFS\nSuppose an economy can produce either 100 units of food and 0 machines, or 80 food and 10 machines, or 50 food and 20 machines.\n• First 10 machines cost 20 food units.\n• Next 10 machines cost 30 food units.\nThe opportunity cost of additional machines increases, illustrating a bowed-out PPC.\n\nAn economy can move from an interior point toward its frontier by employing idle resources more efficiently WITHOUT new technology. A frontier shifts outward only when productive capacity improves. JAMB often tests this distinction.\n\n14. SHORT RUN VS LONG RUN\nThe short run is a period in which at least one input is fixed; the long run is a period in which all inputs can be varied. These are economic definitions, not fixed numbers of days. A bakery may add workers quickly but need much longer to build another factory.\n\n15. HOW TO ANSWER A JAMB SCENARIO\nIf a question says “The government must choose between constructing a bridge and a hospital,” identify scarcity and opportunity cost.\nIf it says “Factories have unused machines and unemployed workers,” think a point INSIDE the PPC.\nIf it says “A new technology doubles output with the same inputs,” think an OUTWARD PPC shift.\nIf it says “Should government raise the minimum wage?”, think normative judgement.\n\nCHECK YOUR REASONING\nA government spends ₦50 million on a new clinic rather than repairing a road. Is the opportunity cost ₦50 million? Not necessarily. The opportunity cost is the value of the next-best forgone use of those resources, which may be the road repair. Monetary expenditure and opportunity cost answer different questions.\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Can a billionaire experience scarcity? YES. Money and time remain limited relative to possible wants.\n2. A student chooses A over B and C; B was second best. What is the opportunity cost? B.\n3. What does a point inside the PPC show? Unused or inefficiently employed resources.\n4. What shifts a PPC outward? Greater productive capacity, such as improved technology.\n5. “Government ought to subsidise transport.” Positive or normative? Normative.\n6. Is Nigeria purely capitalist or purely centrally planned? Neither; it operates a mixed economy."}},{"id":"tools-analysis","old":{"title":"Tools of economic analysis","body":"Economists present and analyse data with tables, graphs, charts and simple statistics.\n\nPresenting data:\n• Tables: rows and columns, e.g. a demand schedule.\n• Graphs: a line graph plots one variable against another (price on the vertical axis, quantity on the horizontal in demand and supply). The slope shows the rate of change.\n• Bar chart: bars of equal width, height proportional to value; component (subdivided) bar charts show parts of a total.\n• Pie chart: a circle divided into sectors. Angle of a sector = (item ÷ total) × 360°.\n• Histogram: bars for grouped (continuous) data with no gaps; the tallest bar is the modal class.\n• Pictogram: pictures, each standing for a fixed quantity.\n\nMeasures of central tendency:\n• Mean (arithmetic average) = sum of values ÷ number of values: x̄ = Σx ÷ n. For a frequency table, x̄ = Σfx ÷ Σf.\n• Median: the middle value when the data are arranged in order. For n items, position = (n + 1) ÷ 2.\n• Mode: the value that occurs most often. Data can be bimodal.\n\nWorked example: the weekly sales (₦'000) of a kiosk are 12, 15, 9, 15, 20, 11, 16.\nMean = (12 + 15 + 9 + 15 + 20 + 11 + 16) ÷ 7 = 98 ÷ 7 = 14, i.e. ₦14,000.\nOrdered: 9, 11, 12, 15, 15, 16, 20; median = 4th value = 15 (₦15,000).\nMode = 15 (₦15,000). Range = 20 − 9 = 11 (₦11,000).\n\nPie chart example: a family spends ₦30,000 on food out of ₦120,000: angle = (30,000 ÷ 120,000) × 360° = 90°.\n\nMeasures of dispersion (spread):\n• Range = highest − lowest value.\n• Mean deviation = Σ|x − x̄| ÷ n.\n• Variance = Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ n; standard deviation = √variance.\nExample: 2, 4, 6. Mean = 4; deviations −2, 0, 2; squares 4, 0, 4; variance = 8 ÷ 3 = 2.67; standard deviation = √2.67 ≈ 1.63; mean deviation = (2 + 0 + 2) ÷ 3 = 1.33.\n\nIndex numbers compare a value with a base year (= 100):\n• Simple price index = (current price ÷ base-year price) × 100.\nExample: a bag of rice costs ₦40,000 in the base year and ₦52,000 now: index = (52,000 ÷ 40,000) × 100 = 130, a 30% rise.\n• Weighted index: Σ(price relative × weight) ÷ Σweights. The Consumer Price Index is a weighted index; the NBS uses it to measure inflation.\n\nMerits of the mean: uses all values; demerit: distorted by extreme values. The median and mode are not affected by extremes.\n\nCommon traps:\n• In the median, arrange the figures in order FIRST.\n• Pie chart angles add up to 360°, not 100.\n• An index of 130 means a 30% rise, not 130%.\n• The modal class in a histogram is the tallest bar, not the widest interval or the middle one.\n• Use the correct base: percentage change = (new − old) ÷ old × 100."},"new":{"title":"Tools of economic analysis","body":"TOOLS OF ECONOMIC ANALYSIS — READ, CALCULATE AND INTERPRET\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB uses tables, graphs, percentages, averages and index numbers to test whether you can reason from data. Learn the meaning of each tool and practise the calculations.\n\n1. TABLES AND SCHEDULES\nA table organises observations in rows and columns. A demand schedule, for example, may list the quantities consumers buy at different prices.\n\nEXAMPLE — DEMAND SCHEDULE\nPrice (₦):          100 | 200 | 300\nQuantity demanded:   30 |  20 |  10\n\nAs price rises, quantity demanded falls, other things being equal. A table shows the numerical relationship; a graph makes its direction easier to see.\n\n2. GRAPHS: WHAT THE AXES MEAN\nFor conventional economics demand and supply diagrams:\n• Vertical (Y) axis = price.\n• Horizontal (X) axis = quantity.\n• Downward-sloping demand curve = inverse relationship.\n• Upward-sloping supply curve = direct relationship, other things equal.\n\nA movement ALONG a curve occurs when the good's own price changes. A SHIFT of the whole curve reflects another determinant, such as income, tastes or production costs.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Do not call a rise in quantity demanded an “increase in demand” when the cause is only a lower own price.\n\n3. CHOOSING THE RIGHT CHART\n• Bar chart: compares separate categories such as output across states. Bars usually have gaps.\n• Histogram: shows frequencies for continuous grouped data; bars touch. With unequal class widths, use frequency density for bar heights.\n• Pie chart: shows each category's share of a whole; all angles sum to 360°.\n• Line graph: displays trends over time or relationships between variables.\n• Frequency table: shows how often each observation or interval occurs.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE — PIE CHART\nA household spends 15% of its budget on transport.\nSector angle = (15 ÷ 100) × 360° = 54°.\nIf food accounts for 40%, its angle = 144°.\nCheck: all category percentages must sum to 100%.\n\n4. PERCENTAGES AND PERCENTAGE CHANGE\nPercentage = (part ÷ whole) × 100.\nPercentage change = [(new value − old value) ÷ old value] × 100.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA commodity's price increases from ₦80 to ₦100.\nChange = ₦20.\nPercentage increase = (20 ÷ 80) × 100 = 25%, NOT 20%.\nThe denominator is the ORIGINAL value.\n\nIf price then falls from ₦100 to ₦80, the fall is (20 ÷ 100) × 100 = 20%. A 25% rise followed by a 20% fall returns to the starting price.\n\n5. AVERAGES — MEAN, MEDIAN AND MODE\nData: 2, 3, 3, 4, 18.\n• Mean = (2 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 18) ÷ 5 = 6.\n• Median = middle observation after arranging values = 3.\n• Mode = most frequent observation = 3.\n• Range = highest − lowest = 18 − 2 = 16.\n\nThe mean is sensitive to extreme values; the median is often more representative for skewed income distributions.\n\nEVEN NUMBER OF OBSERVATIONS: For 2, 4, 6, 10, median = (4 + 6) ÷ 2 = 5.\n\n6. WEIGHTED MEAN\nSome observations count more than others. Weighted mean = sum of (value × weight) ÷ sum of weights.\n\nEXAMPLE\nA student scores 70 on a test worth 40% and 80 on an exam worth 60%.\nWeighted score = (70 × 0.4) + (80 × 0.6) = 76.\nDo not simply average 70 and 80 unless both carry equal weights.\n\n7. INDEX NUMBERS\nAn index compares a value with a base period, usually assigned 100.\nSimple price index = (current price ÷ base-period price) × 100.\n\nEXAMPLE\nBase-year price ₦200; current price ₦250.\nIndex = (250 ÷ 200) × 100 = 125.\nInterpretation: the price is 25% ABOVE the base-period level, not 125% above it.\n\nAn index of 90 means 10% below the base. A consumer price index (CPI) summarises changes in prices of a representative basket, using weights.\n\n8. SIMPLE RELATIONSHIPS AND EQUATIONS\nA linear relationship may be written Qd = a − bP, where a is the intercept and b is the positive coefficient showing how quantity demanded falls as price rises.\n\nEXAMPLE\nQd = 50 − 2P. At P = 10, Qd = 50 − 20 = 30.\nAt P = 15, Qd = 20.\nInterpret the result before using it: quantities should be economically meaningful.\n\n9. CORRELATION IS NOT CAUSATION\nTwo variables may move together without one causing the other. Ice-cream sales and swimming incidents may both rise in hot weather; heat is a relevant third factor.\n\nJAMB EXAM CHECKLIST\n• Read chart labels and units.\n• Identify the base value before calculating a percentage.\n• Arrange data before finding a median.\n• Distinguish a histogram from a bar chart.\n• Interpret an index relative to 100.\n• Do not infer causation from correlation alone.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — WORKING WITH ECONOMIC DATA\n\n10. FREQUENCY DISTRIBUTIONS\nA frequency distribution groups observations and counts their occurrences. For the data 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, the frequency of 4 is three, while the total frequency is seven.\n\nFor grouped observations, the estimated mean is:\nMean ≈ Σ(f × class midpoint) ÷ Σf.\nUse the midpoint of each class interval, multiply by its frequency, add the products and divide by the total frequency.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nScores 0–9: frequency 2, midpoint 4.5.\nScores 10–19: frequency 3, midpoint 14.5.\nEstimated mean = [(2 × 4.5) + (3 × 14.5)] ÷ 5\n= (9 + 43.5) ÷ 5 = 10.5.\nIt is an estimate because the individual observations within each interval are unknown.\n\n11. CUMULATIVE FREQUENCY\nCumulative frequency is the running total of frequencies. If three classes have frequencies 4, 6 and 5, cumulative frequencies are 4, 10 and 15. The final cumulative frequency equals the total number of observations.\n\nAn ogive is a cumulative-frequency curve. It can be used to estimate medians, quartiles and percentiles from grouped data.\n\n12. MEASURES OF DISPERSION\nCentral tendency tells us the typical value; dispersion tells us how spread out the values are.\n• Range = maximum − minimum.\n• Variance = average squared deviation from the mean, with population and sample formulas differing in their denominators.\n• Standard deviation = square root of variance.\n• A larger standard deviation generally indicates greater dispersion when comparing observations measured on the same scale.\n\nEXAMPLE: Two classes may have the same average test score of 60, but one has scores tightly clustered near 60 while the other ranges from 20 to 100. Their means match; their dispersion does not.\n\n13. REAL AND NOMINAL VALUES\nNominal values are measured in current money terms. Real values adjust for price-level changes.\n\nSimplified deflation:\nReal value at base-year prices ≈ (nominal value ÷ price index) × 100, when the index base is 100.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nNominal income = ₦150,000; relevant price index = 125.\nReal income in base-year purchasing-power terms ≈ ₦150,000 ÷ 125 × 100 = ₦120,000.\nA higher nominal income does not necessarily mean higher purchasing power if prices rise faster.\n\n14. SIMPLE WEIGHTED PRICE INDEX\nSuppose a basket contains 2 units of rice and 1 unit of oil.\nBase prices: rice ₦100, oil ₦200 → basket ₦400.\nCurrent prices: rice ₦150, oil ₦250 → same basket ₦550.\nFixed-basket index = (550 ÷ 400) × 100 = 137.5.\nInterpretation: the basket costs 37.5% more than in the base period. This illustrates the principle of a Laspeyres-type fixed-basket index.\n\n15. CAUTION WITH GRAPH SCALES\nA bar chart whose vertical axis starts at 90 rather than zero can exaggerate differences. Read the numerical axis before comparing visual heights. For a line graph, check whether the horizontal axis represents equal time intervals.\n\n16. FIVE COMMON CALCULATION ERRORS\n• Using the NEW value rather than the OLD value as the percentage-change denominator.\n• Forgetting to sort values before finding the median.\n• Confusing frequency with cumulative frequency.\n• Interpreting an index of 140 as a 140% increase rather than 40%.\n• Using a simple average where observations have different weights.\n\nEXAM PRACTICE — SOLVE BEFORE READING\nA commodity's price increases from ₦240 to ₦300. Calculate the percentage increase.\nSolution: (300 − 240)/240 × 100 = 25%.\nA pie chart shows 25% of household spending on rent. Angle?\nSolution: 0.25 × 360° = 90°.\nScores are 4, 7, 8, 9, 22. Which is more resistant to the extreme value 22?\nSolution: median (8), not mean (10).\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Price rises from ₦80 to ₦100. Percentage rise? 25%.\n2. What pie-chart angle represents 15%? 54°.\n3. Find the median of 2, 3, 3, 4, 18. Answer: 3.\n4. Base price ₦100, current price ₦125. Index? 125, meaning 25% higher.\n5. Which average is most affected by an extreme value? Mean.\n6. In a standard demand graph, which axis carries price? Vertical axis."}},{"id":"demand-supply","old":{"title":"Demand, supply and price determination","body":"Demand is the quantity of a good consumers are willing and able to buy at a given price over a period. Desire without ability to pay is not effective demand.\n\nLaw of demand: ceteris paribus, the higher the price, the lower the quantity demanded, and the lower the price, the higher the quantity demanded. The demand curve slopes downward from left to right.\n\nDeterminants of demand (they SHIFT the curve): income, prices of related goods (substitutes and complements), tastes and fashion, population, expectations of future prices, advertising, government policy (taxes, subsidies), season and weather.\n• Change in quantity demanded = movement ALONG the curve, caused only by the good's own price.\n• Change in demand (increase or decrease) = SHIFT of the whole curve.\n\nTypes: joint (complementary) demand, e.g. cars and petrol; competitive demand (substitutes), e.g. Peak and Three Crowns milk; derived demand, e.g. labour or cement derived from demand for houses; composite demand, e.g. palm oil for cooking and soap.\n\nExceptional demand curves (slope upward): Giffen goods, goods of ostentation (Veblen goods, e.g. luxury cars), expectation of further price rises, and ignorance.\n\nSupply is the quantity producers are willing and able to offer at a given price. Law of supply: the higher the price, the higher the quantity supplied; the curve slopes upward. Determinants: cost of production, technology, prices of other goods, weather (for farm produce), number of firms, taxes and subsidies, goals of the firm. Joint supply: beef and hides; competitive supply: land used for maize or cassava.\n\nEquilibrium: where quantity demanded = quantity supplied. Below equilibrium price there is excess demand (shortage) and price rises; above it there is excess supply (surplus) and price falls.\nWorked example: Qd = 100 − 2P and Qs = 20 + 2P. At equilibrium 100 − 2P = 20 + 2P, so 80 = 4P, P = ₦20; Q = 100 − 40 = 60 units.\n\nShifts: an increase in demand raises both price and quantity; an increase in supply lowers price and raises quantity.\n\nPrice control:\n• Maximum price (price ceiling), set BELOW equilibrium to protect consumers: causes shortage, queues, rationing, hoarding and black markets (e.g. controlled petrol prices in Nigeria before deregulation in 2023).\n• Minimum price (price floor), set ABOVE equilibrium to protect producers or workers: causes surplus, which government may buy and store. The national minimum wage is a price floor in the labour market.\n• Rationing: sharing scarce goods by coupons, quotas or queues.\n\nCommon traps:\n• \"Demand falls\" (shift) is not the same as \"quantity demanded falls\" (movement).\n• A ceiling only has effect below equilibrium, a floor only above it.\n• A rise in the price of a substitute RAISES demand for the other good; a rise in the price of a complement LOWERS it.\n• The law of supply is a DIRECT relationship; the law of demand is INVERSE.\n• Income is a determinant of demand, not of supply."},"new":{"title":"Demand, supply and price determination","body":"DEMAND, SUPPLY AND PRICE DETERMINATION — FOLLOW THE CAUSE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB repeatedly tests whether a change is a movement ALONG a curve or a SHIFT of the curve. Begin every question by asking: What changed first?\n\n1. DEMAND: WILLING AND ABLE TO BUY\nDemand is the quantity consumers are willing AND able to purchase at specified prices over a period. Wanting a phone without purchasing power is not effective demand.\n\nLaw of demand: other things equal (ceteris paribus), a higher price leads to lower quantity demanded, and a lower price to higher quantity demanded.\n\nEXAMPLE — OWN-PRICE MOVEMENT\nIf a bag of rice becomes cheaper and consumers buy more rice, that is an INCREASE IN QUANTITY DEMANDED: movement down along the existing demand curve.\n\n2. WHAT SHIFTS DEMAND?\nA change in something OTHER than the good's own price can shift the whole demand curve.\n• Income: demand for a normal good generally rises as income rises; demand for an inferior good may fall.\n• Tastes, fashion and advertising.\n• Prices of substitutes: if tea becomes dearer, demand for coffee may increase.\n• Prices of complements: if printers become dearer, demand for ink cartridges may decrease.\n• Population or number of buyers.\n• Expectations of future prices or income.\n• Seasonal conditions.\n\nA RIGHTWARD demand shift means more is demanded at every given price. A LEFTWARD shift means less.\n\nJAMB TRAP: “Increase in demand” means a shift right. “Increase in quantity demanded” means movement along the same curve.\n\n3. SUPPLY: WILLING AND ABLE TO SELL\nSupply is the quantity producers are willing and able to offer for sale at different prices over a period.\n\nLaw of supply: other things equal, a higher price generally encourages a larger quantity supplied. The usual supply curve slopes upward.\n\nEXAMPLE — OWN-PRICE MOVEMENT\nIf the price of tomatoes rises and farmers bring more tomatoes to market, quantity supplied increases along the supply curve.\n\n4. WHAT SHIFTS SUPPLY?\n• Production costs: higher fuel or fertiliser costs tend to shift supply LEFT.\n• Technology: better equipment can shift supply RIGHT.\n• Taxes on production generally shift supply LEFT; subsidies may shift it RIGHT.\n• Weather and natural conditions affect farm output.\n• Number of producers and availability of inputs.\n• Expectations and prices of alternative products.\n\nA shift right means producers can offer more at each price; a shift left means less.\n\n5. MARKET EQUILIBRIUM — WHERE PLANS MATCH\nEquilibrium occurs where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nSuppose Qd = 100 − 2P and Qs = 20 + 2P.\nSet Qd = Qs:\n100 − 2P = 20 + 2P\n80 = 4P\nEquilibrium price P = 20.\nEquilibrium quantity Q = 100 − 2(20) = 60.\nCheck supply: 20 + 2(20) = 60.\n\nAt a price ABOVE equilibrium, quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded: SURPLUS. Sellers face pressure to lower prices.\nAt a price BELOW equilibrium, quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied: SHORTAGE. Buyers compete for limited stock, creating upward price pressure.\n\n6. PREDICTING A NEW EQUILIBRIUM\n• Demand rises, supply unchanged → equilibrium PRICE rises and QUANTITY rises.\n• Demand falls, supply unchanged → price falls and quantity falls.\n• Supply rises, demand unchanged → price falls and quantity rises.\n• Supply falls, demand unchanged → price rises and quantity falls.\n\nIf demand and supply both shift, the effect on price or quantity may be uncertain without knowing their relative sizes.\n\nEXAMPLE: Better harvests increase tomato supply. Other things equal, market price falls and quantity sold rises.\n\n7. PRICE CEILINGS AND FLOORS\nA price CEILING is a legal maximum. If set BELOW equilibrium and enforced, it tends to create a shortage. Example: a rent cap below the market-clearing rent may generate excess demand for available housing.\n\nA price FLOOR is a legal minimum. If set ABOVE equilibrium and enforced, it tends to create a surplus. Example: a minimum guaranteed agricultural price may encourage output beyond purchases.\n\nA ceiling above equilibrium or floor below equilibrium is non-binding and need not change the market outcome.\n\n8. RELATED GOODS\n• Substitutes: consumers can use one instead of the other (tea and coffee).\n• Complements: goods used together (cars and petrol).\n• Normal goods: demand usually rises with income.\n• Inferior goods: demand usually falls as income rises, all else equal.\n\nEXAM TRAP: “Inferior” describes the income–demand relationship; it does not necessarily mean low quality.\n\n9. EXCEPTIONS AND LIMITATIONS\nThe law of demand assumes other factors remain constant. Rare Giffen-good behaviour and some status-driven Veblen-good purchases are discussed as exceptions or special cases, but do not assume every expensive luxury violates the law of demand.\n\nQUICK DIAGNOSTIC\nAsk three questions:\n1. Did the good's OWN price change? Think movement along the curve.\n2. Did another factor change? Think curve shift.\n3. Which curve shifted and in which direction? Then predict equilibrium.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — MULTIPLE SHIFTS AND MARKET POLICY\n\n10. DERIVING EQUILIBRIUM FROM A TABLE\nSuppose a market has the following schedules:\nPrice ₦10: quantity demanded 90, quantity supplied 30.\nPrice ₦20: quantity demanded 70, quantity supplied 50.\nPrice ₦30: quantity demanded 50, quantity supplied 50.\nPrice ₦40: quantity demanded 30, quantity supplied 70.\n\nAt ₦30, buyers and sellers each plan to trade 50 units. This is equilibrium.\nAt ₦20, excess demand = 70 − 50 = 20 units (shortage).\nAt ₦40, excess supply = 70 − 30 = 40 units (surplus).\n\n11. SIMULTANEOUS CHANGES — WHAT CAN WE KNOW?\nIf demand AND supply both rise:\n• Equilibrium quantity definitely rises under standard upward supply/downward demand curves.\n• Equilibrium price may rise, fall or remain unchanged depending on the relative shifts.\n\nIf demand rises while supply falls:\n• Equilibrium price definitely rises.\n• Equilibrium quantity is uncertain.\n\nIf demand falls while supply rises:\n• Equilibrium price definitely falls.\n• Equilibrium quantity is uncertain.\n\nIf both demand and supply fall:\n• Equilibrium quantity definitely falls.\n• Equilibrium price is uncertain.\n\nThis is a favourite reasoning trap: NEVER claim both price and quantity outcomes are certain when opposing shifts make one ambiguous.\n\n12. DIRECT, DERIVED, JOINT AND COMPETITIVE DEMAND\n• Direct demand: a consumer wants a good for its own use, such as bread.\n• Derived demand: demand for an input depends on demand for the final product, such as labour to build houses.\n• Joint demand: complementary goods are demanded together, such as cars and fuel.\n• Competitive demand: substitutes compete for buyers, such as tea and coffee.\n\nJOINT SUPPLY occurs when one productive process yields multiple outputs, such as beef and hides from cattle. Do not confuse joint demand (goods used together) with joint supply (products produced together).\n\n13. MARKET STRUCTURE AND PRICE SETTING\nIn a highly competitive market, individual sellers have limited ability to set prices independently. In a monopoly, one seller may have greater pricing power, although buyers' willingness to pay and substitutes still constrain demand.\n\n“Price taker” describes a firm that accepts the market price; “price maker” describes a firm with some control over its selling price. Do not assume every large firm is a monopoly.\n\n14. TAXES, SUBSIDIES AND INCIDENCE\nA per-unit tax on sellers raises the cost of supplying each unit and shifts supply upward/left in the standard diagram. Buyers typically pay a higher price, sellers receive a lower net-of-tax price, and equilibrium quantity falls.\n\nWHO BEARS THE TAX?\nThe less price-responsive side of the market generally bears more of the tax burden. If demand is very inelastic relative to supply, consumers tend to bear more; if supply is very inelastic relative to demand, producers tend to bear more.\n\nA production subsidy generally shifts supply right, tending to lower the consumer price and increase quantity, other things equal.\n\n15. GOVERNMENT PRICE CONTROLS — CONSEQUENCES\nA binding ceiling can create queues, rationing or unofficial markets because the quantity demanded exceeds the quantity offered. A binding floor can create unsold stocks or require public purchases if government guarantees the minimum price.\n\nPolicy goals matter: a rent ceiling may seek affordability, but its effects depend on housing supply, enforcement and complementary policies. The simple model predicts pressures, not every real-world detail.\n\n16. APPLY THE LOGIC TO NIGERIA\n• Higher fertiliser prices: supply of farm produce may shift left.\n• Better roads reducing transport losses: supply available in urban markets may shift right.\n• Rising incomes: demand for normal goods may shift right.\n• An increase in the price of a close substitute: demand for the other product may shift right.\n\nTEST YOURSELF — REASON, DON'T GUESS\nA flood destroys part of a tomato harvest while household demand stays unchanged. What happens?\nAnswer: supply shifts left; equilibrium price rises and quantity falls.\nDemand and supply both increase. What must rise?\nAnswer: equilibrium quantity; the price effect is uncertain.\nA tax raises the price paid by consumers by ₦30 but sellers' net receipt falls ₦20. What is the per-unit tax?\nAnswer: ₦50, the wedge between the two prices.\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Rice becomes cheaper and consumers buy more. Demand shift or movement? Movement along demand: higher quantity demanded.\n2. Tea becomes dearer. What may happen to coffee demand? It shifts right if coffee is a substitute.\n3. A binding maximum price below equilibrium causes what? Shortage.\n4. A binding minimum price above equilibrium causes what? Surplus.\n5. Better farming technology does what to supply? Shifts supply right.\n6. If demand increases while supply stays fixed, what happens? Equilibrium price and quantity rise."}},{"id":"elasticity","old":{"title":"Elasticity of demand and supply","body":"Elasticity measures how responsive quantity is to a change in one of its determinants.\n\n1. Price elasticity of demand (PED) = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price (ignore the minus sign).\n% change = (change ÷ original value) × 100.\nWorked example: price of a loaf rises from ₦1,000 to ₦1,200; quantity demanded falls from 50 to 35.\nStep 1: % change in Qd = (15 ÷ 50) × 100 = 30%.\nStep 2: % change in P = (200 ÷ 1,000) × 100 = 20%.\nStep 3: PED = 30 ÷ 20 = 1.5 (elastic).\nTypes: PED > 1 elastic; PED < 1 inelastic; PED = 1 unitary; PED = 0 perfectly inelastic (vertical curve); PED = ∞ perfectly elastic (horizontal curve).\nTotal revenue test: if demand is elastic, a price rise reduces total revenue; if inelastic, a price rise increases it; if unitary, total revenue is unchanged. In the example, TR before = ₦1,000 × 50 = ₦50,000 and after = ₦1,200 × 35 = ₦42,000: TR falls, as expected for elastic demand.\nDeterminants of PED: availability of substitutes, whether a necessity or luxury, proportion of income spent, number of uses, habit (cigarettes), time period, durability.\n\n2. Income elasticity of demand (YED) = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in income. Positive for normal goods (above 1 for luxuries), negative for inferior goods.\n\n3. Cross elasticity of demand (XED) = % change in quantity demanded of good X ÷ % change in price of good Y. Positive for substitutes, negative for complements, zero for unrelated goods.\n\n4. Price elasticity of supply (PES) = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price. Perfectly inelastic supply is vertical (land, a stadium's seats); perfectly elastic supply is horizontal. Supply is more elastic with time, spare capacity, storable goods and mobile factors. Farm produce supply is inelastic in the short run because crops take time to grow.\n\nIncidence of tax: who finally bears an indirect tax.\n• If demand is inelastic (or supply elastic), consumers bear most of the tax.\n• If demand is elastic (or supply inelastic), producers bear most.\n• Perfectly inelastic demand: consumers bear all; perfectly elastic demand: producers bear all.\nExample: a ₦100 tax per crate raises the market price from ₦2,000 to ₦2,070. Consumers bear ₦70 and producers ₦30.\n\nUses of elasticity: government chooses goods with inelastic demand to tax for revenue; firms use it for pricing and price discrimination; it explains the effects of devaluation.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Always divide by the ORIGINAL value when computing % change.\n• PED is quantity over price, not price over quantity.\n• A vertical demand or supply curve is perfectly INELASTIC.\n• Negative XED means complements; negative YED means inferior goods.\n• Inelastic demand plus a price rise means total revenue rises."},"new":{"title":"Elasticity of demand and supply","body":"ELASTICITY — MEASURE HOW STRONGLY BUYERS AND SELLERS RESPOND\n\n1. WHAT ELASTICITY MEANS\nElasticity is RESPONSIVENESS, not simply a change in quantity. It measures the percentage change in one variable caused by a percentage change in another.\n\n2. PRICE ELASTICITY OF DEMAND (PED)\nPED = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price.\nDemand normally moves in the opposite direction to price, so PED is often negative. In JAMB classifications, use its ABSOLUTE value unless the question asks for the signed coefficient.\n\n• Elastic: |PED| > 1 — quantity changes proportionately more than price.\n• Inelastic: |PED| < 1 — quantity changes proportionately less.\n• Unit elastic: |PED| = 1.\n• Perfectly inelastic: PED = 0, a vertical demand curve.\n• Perfectly elastic: an idealised horizontal demand curve, PED tending to infinity.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE — ORIGINAL-BASE METHOD\nPrice rises from ₦1,000 to ₦1,200; quantity falls from 50 to 35.\n% change in price = 200/1,000 × 100 = 20%.\n% change in quantity = −15/50 × 100 = −30%.\nPED = −30%/20% = −1.5. Absolute value 1.5: elastic.\n\nMIDPOINT METHOD: Some questions specify arc or midpoint elasticity. Then divide each change by the AVERAGE of old and new values, not the original. Always follow the question's stated method.\n\n3. ELASTICITY AND TOTAL REVENUE\nTotal revenue (TR) = price × quantity sold.\n• Elastic demand: a price CUT generally raises TR; a price rise lowers TR.\n• Inelastic demand: a price RISE generally raises TR; a price cut lowers TR.\n• Unit elasticity: small price changes leave TR approximately unchanged.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nInitially 50 units sell at ₦1,000: TR = ₦50,000.\nAfter the price rises to ₦1,200, 35 sell: TR = ₦42,000.\nRevenue falls, consistent with elastic demand over this change.\n\n4. WHAT DETERMINES PED?\n• Availability of close substitutes: more substitutes usually means more elastic demand.\n• Necessity versus luxury: necessities tend to be less elastic.\n• Share of consumer income: expensive items may be more elastic.\n• Time to adjust: demand often becomes more elastic over longer periods.\n• How narrowly a product is defined: one brand usually has more substitutes than an entire product category.\n\nEXAM TRAP: Inelastic does NOT mean demand never changes; it means the percentage response is smaller than the percentage price change.\n\n5. INCOME ELASTICITY OF DEMAND (YED)\nYED = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in income.\n• Positive: normal good.\n• Negative: inferior good.\n• Greater than 1: income-elastic demand, often associated with luxuries.\n• Between 0 and 1: income-inelastic normal demand, often associated with necessities.\n\nEXAMPLE: Income rises 10%; demand for a service rises 20%. YED = +2. It is a normal, income-elastic good.\n\n6. CROSS-PRICE ELASTICITY (XED)\nXED = % change in demand for good A ÷ % change in price of good B.\n• Positive: substitutes, such as tea and coffee.\n• Negative: complements, such as printers and ink.\n• Near zero: weakly related goods.\n\nEXAMPLE: Tea price rises 10%; coffee demand rises 5%. XED = +0.5: substitutes.\n\n7. PRICE ELASTICITY OF SUPPLY (PES)\nPES = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price.\nSupply is usually positively related to price.\n• Elastic supply: PES > 1.\n• Inelastic supply: PES < 1.\n• Perfectly inelastic supply: PES = 0 (vertical curve).\n• Perfectly elastic supply: idealised horizontal curve.\n\nSupply tends to be more elastic when firms have spare capacity, available inputs, stocks and enough time to expand production. Fresh farm produce may be relatively inelastic in the short run.\n\n8. GRAPH AND EXAM WARNINGS\nA STEEP curve often suggests relative inelasticity and a FLAT curve relative elasticity, but slope and elasticity are not identical concepts: elasticity also depends on the price–quantity point and measurement units. On a straight-line demand curve, PED varies along the line.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — ELASTICITY IN MARKETS AND EXAMS\n\n9. POINT ELASTICITY VS ARC ELASTICITY\nThe original-base percentage method measures responsiveness using a chosen starting point. Reversing the direction of a price change can produce a different numerical percentage.\n\nThe midpoint (arc) method avoids this direction problem for two observations:\nArc PED = [(Q2 − Q1)/((Q1 + Q2)/2)] ÷ [(P2 − P1)/((P1 + P2)/2)].\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nPrice rises from ₦10 to ₦12; quantity falls from 100 to 80.\nQuantity percentage change using midpoint = −20/90 = −22.22%.\nPrice percentage change using midpoint = 2/11 = 18.18%.\nArc PED ≈ −1.22; magnitude 1.22, elastic.\n\nCompare with original-base method: −20%/20% = −1, unit elastic. The answers differ because the METHODS differ. Use the one the exam specifies.\n\n10. ELASTICITY ALONG A STRAIGHT-LINE DEMAND CURVE\nFor a downward-sloping straight-line demand curve, elasticity is not constant along the curve:\n• Upper portion (high price, low quantity): generally elastic.\n• Midpoint: unit elastic.\n• Lower portion (low price, high quantity): generally inelastic.\n\nDo not confuse a constant SLOPE with constant ELASTICITY. Elasticity incorporates proportional changes and therefore depends on the point.\n\n11. DEMAND ELASTICITY AND REVENUE — WHY IT MATTERS\nSuppose a cinema sells 100 tickets at ₦2,000 each: revenue ₦200,000.\nIt lowers the price to ₦1,800 and sells 130 tickets: revenue ₦234,000.\nThe price reduction increased revenue because the quantity response was strong enough.\n\nBut revenue is not profit. If serving more customers raises costs substantially, greater revenue need not mean greater profit. JAMB questions about “total expenditure” or “total revenue” are asking about price × quantity, not necessarily profit.\n\n12. SUPPLY ELASTICITY AND TIME\nA farmer cannot immediately grow more yams when prices rise today. Over another planting season, land and inputs can be reallocated, making supply more responsive.\n\nFor a manufactured product, spare factory capacity may allow a quick output increase. If all machines are fully utilised and skilled workers are scarce, supply may be relatively inelastic even when prices rise.\n\n13. ELASTICITY AND TAX POLICY\nIf demand is highly inelastic, a tax-induced price increase may reduce purchases only modestly, although consumers may face a greater burden. If demand is elastic, the same tax may cause a larger contraction in quantity.\n\nCAUTION: “Government should tax inelastic goods” is a policy judgement, not a universal recommendation. Health effects, fairness and distributional impacts also matter.\n\n14. PRICE ELASTICITY OF DEMAND VS SLOPE\nSlope in a standard price-on-vertical-axis demand graph is change in price divided by change in quantity. PED compares PERCENTAGE changes in quantity and price. A graph's apparent steepness depends on its axes and scales; it is not enough to calculate elasticity numerically.\n\n15. EXAM DECISION TREE\n• Question changes the good's own price and asks about its quantity demanded → PED.\n• Question changes consumer income → YED.\n• Question changes the price of a DIFFERENT good → XED.\n• Question changes the price received by producers and asks about quantity supplied → PES.\n• Question asks whether revenue rises after a price cut → consider PED magnitude.\n\nFURTHER WORKED CHECKS\nA good's demand rises 12% when income rises 8%. YED = 12/8 = +1.5: normal and income-elastic.\nDemand for A falls 6% when price of B rises 10%. XED = −0.6: complements.\nSupply rises 5% after price rises 20%. PES = 0.25: inelastic.\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Price rises 10%; quantity demanded falls 30%. PED magnitude? 3, elastic.\n2. What happens to revenue when price rises under elastic demand? It generally falls.\n3. Income rises 20%; demand falls 10%. YED? −0.5, inferior good.\n4. Cross-price elasticity is positive. Relationship? Substitutes.\n5. Why is housing supply often inelastic in the short run? Construction takes time.\n6. PED equals zero. What is the curve? Vertical; perfectly inelastic demand."}},{"id":"consumer-behaviour","old":{"title":"Theory of consumer behaviour","body":"Utility is the satisfaction a consumer gets from consuming a good or service. It is subjective and, in cardinal theory, measured in imaginary units called utils.\n\n• Total utility (TU): the total satisfaction from all units consumed.\n• Marginal utility (MU): the extra satisfaction from one more unit: MU = change in TU ÷ change in quantity.\n• Average utility = TU ÷ quantity.\n\nLaw of diminishing marginal utility: as a consumer takes more and more units of a good in a period, other things being equal, the marginal utility of each additional unit eventually falls.\nWorked example (sachets of water):\nUnits: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\nTU: 10, 18, 24, 27, 27\nMU: 10, 8, 6, 3, 0\nTU is maximum (27) when MU = 0; if a 6th sachet gave TU of 25, MU would be −2 (disutility).\nAssumptions: units are consumed continuously, are of the same size and quality, and tastes and income are unchanged.\n\nConsumer equilibrium (one good): the consumer buys until MU = price (in money terms).\nEqui-marginal principle (many goods): the consumer is in equilibrium when\nMUa ÷ Pa = MUb ÷ Pb = … = MUn ÷ Pn, with all income spent.\nExample: if MU of rice = 40 at ₦20 per unit and MU of beans = 30 at ₦10, then MU/P is 2 for rice and 3 for beans, so the consumer should buy more beans (lowering its MU) and less rice until the ratios are equal.\n\nThe law of diminishing MU explains why the demand curve slopes downward: a consumer will pay less for extra units that give less satisfaction.\n\nParadox of value (water and diamonds): water has high total utility but low marginal utility because it is plentiful; diamonds are scarce so their marginal utility and price are high.\n\nConsumer surplus: the difference between what a consumer is willing to pay and what he actually pays. If Ngozi would pay ₦5,000 for a shirt but buys it for ₦3,500, her consumer surplus is ₦1,500. On a graph it is the area below the demand curve and above the price line.\n\nIndifference curve approach (ordinal utility): an indifference curve shows combinations of two goods that give the same satisfaction. Indifference curves slope downward, are convex to the origin (diminishing marginal rate of substitution), never intersect, and higher curves mean more satisfaction. The budget line shows what the consumer can afford. Equilibrium is where the budget line is tangent to the highest attainable indifference curve.\n\nCommon traps:\n• It is MARGINAL utility that diminishes; total utility still rises while MU is positive.\n• TU is at its maximum when MU is zero, not when MU is highest.\n• Consumer surplus = willingness to pay − actual price, not price − cost.\n• Indifference curves cannot cross.\n• Cardinal = measurable utils (marginal utility theory); ordinal = ranking only (indifference curves)."},"new":{"title":"Theory of consumer behaviour","body":"CONSUMER BEHAVIOUR — WHY PEOPLE CHOOSE ONE BASKET OVER ANOTHER\n\n1. UTILITY AND CHOICE\nUtility means satisfaction or benefit derived from consuming goods and services. It is subjective: the same product can be valued differently by different consumers.\n\nCardinal utility theory uses imaginary units called “utils”. Ordinal utility theory ranks bundles without needing to measure satisfaction numerically.\n\n2. TOTAL, MARGINAL AND AVERAGE UTILITY\n• Total utility (TU): total satisfaction from all units consumed.\n• Marginal utility (MU): EXTRA satisfaction from one additional unit.\n• Average utility (AU): TU divided by quantity.\n\nMU = change in TU ÷ change in quantity.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nUnits of oranges: 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5\nTotal utility:      0 | 12 | 22 | 30 | 35 | 35\nMarginal utility:   – | 12 | 10 |  8 |  5 |  0\n\nThe fourth orange adds 5 utils; the fifth adds none. Total utility reaches its maximum where MU is zero, in this example. If MU becomes negative, consuming another unit reduces TU.\n\n3. LAW OF DIMINISHING MARGINAL UTILITY\nAs a consumer consumes successive units of a good within a given period, holding other relevant conditions constant, the ADDITIONAL satisfaction tends to fall.\n\nEXAMPLE: The first glass of water when thirsty may be extremely satisfying. The second helps less; a fifth may provide almost no additional satisfaction.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Diminishing MU does not mean TU must immediately fall. TU can keep rising while MU remains positive but gets smaller.\n\n4. CONSUMER EQUILIBRIUM — CARDINAL APPROACH\nA consumer with a fixed budget seeks maximum total utility.\nFor two divisible goods X and Y, an interior optimum under the standard assumptions satisfies:\nMUx/Px = MUy/Py,\nwhile the budget is exhausted when more spending can increase utility.\n\nInterpretation: the marginal utility obtained per naira spent is equal across goods. If one good yields more marginal utility per naira, the consumer can improve satisfaction by reallocating spending toward it, subject to constraints.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nMU of an extra snack = 20 utils; snack price = ₦200 → 0.10 utils per naira.\nMU of an extra drink = 15 utils; drink price = ₦100 → 0.15 utils per naira.\nAt these margins, the drink yields greater utility per naira; reallocating toward drinks may increase total satisfaction.\n\n5. INDIFFERENCE CURVES — ORDINAL APPROACH\nAn indifference curve joins combinations of two goods that give the consumer the SAME level of satisfaction.\n\nStandard properties under usual preferences:\n• Downward sloping: more of one good compensates for less of the other.\n• Higher curves represent greater satisfaction.\n• Curves do not intersect.\n• Typically convex to the origin because of diminishing marginal rate of substitution (MRS).\n\nMRS describes how much of Y the consumer is willing to give up for an additional unit of X while keeping utility constant.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Different points on the SAME indifference curve provide equal satisfaction, even though their quantities differ.\n\n6. THE BUDGET LINE\nA budget line shows all combinations of two goods that use a consumer's available income at given prices.\n\nEquation: PxX + PyY = M, where M is income.\nIntercepts:\n• Maximum X if only X is bought = M/Px.\n• Maximum Y if only Y is bought = M/Py.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nIncome = ₦1,200; X costs ₦200 and Y costs ₦300.\nMaximum X = 1,200/200 = 6.\nMaximum Y = 1,200/300 = 4.\nA basket of 3X and 2Y costs 3(200) + 2(300) = ₦1,200: exactly affordable.\n\nA basket BELOW the budget line costs less than the available income. A basket ABOVE it is unaffordable.\n\n7. ORDINAL CONSUMER EQUILIBRIUM\nUnder standard smooth, convex preferences, an interior optimum occurs where the highest attainable indifference curve is tangent to the budget line:\nMRSxy = Px/Py.\nAt that point the consumer's willingness to trade one good for the other matches the market trade-off.\n\nNot every optimum is a tangency: a corner solution is possible when the consumer buys none of one good.\n\n8. INCOME AND SUBSTITUTION EFFECTS\nWhen the price of X falls:\n• Substitution effect: X becomes cheaper relative to Y, encouraging substitution toward X.\n• Income effect: purchasing power rises because the same income can now buy more.\n\nFor a normal good, both effects increase demand for X when its price falls. For an inferior good, the income effect works in the opposite direction; in a rare Giffen case it can outweigh the substitution effect.\n\n9. CONSUMER SURPLUS\nConsumer surplus is the difference between what a buyer is WILLING to pay and what they ACTUALLY pay.\n\nEXAMPLE: A buyer would pay ₦5,000 for a book but buys it for ₦3,500. Consumer surplus = ₦1,500.\n\nCOMMON EXAM MIX-UPS\nTU = accumulated satisfaction; MU = additional satisfaction.\nDiminishing MU ≠ falling TU while MU is still positive.\nBudget line = affordability; indifference curve = preferences.\nIncome effect ≠ substitution effect.\nConsumer surplus ≠ producer profit.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — CONSUMER CHOICE STEP BY STEP\n\n10. A FULL MARGINAL-UTILITY TABLE\nConsider consumption of cups of juice:\nQuantity:          0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6\nTotal utility:     0 | 18| 32| 42| 48| 50| 49\nMarginal utility:  – | 18| 14| 10|  6|  2| −1\n\nNotice the progression:\n• TU rises while MU is positive.\n• TU reaches its maximum at quantity 5 in this discrete example, because adding the sixth unit reduces utility.\n• MU turns negative for the sixth unit.\n• Diminishing MU starts before TU stops rising.\n\nIMPORTANT PRECISION: In a smooth continuous model, TU reaches a maximum where MU = 0. In a discrete table, the highest TU can occur even if the last positive MU is above zero and the next MU is negative.\n\n11. TWO GOODS AND A LIMITED BUDGET\nA student has ₦600 to spend on snacks (₦200 each) and drinks (₦100 each). They cannot simply choose the highest total utility from an unaffordable bundle.\n\nSuppose the marginal utilities of successive snacks are 30, 20, 10 and of successive drinks are 18, 14, 10, 6.\nThe marginal utility per naira of the first snack is 30/200 = 0.15.\nThe first drink gives 18/100 = 0.18.\nThe second drink gives 14/100 = 0.14.\nThe second snack gives 20/200 = 0.10.\n\nA rational allocation compares the NEXT available marginal utility per naira at each step, subject to the whole budget and indivisibility of units. The familiar equality MUx/Px = MUy/Py is a continuous, interior condition, not a rule that every discrete purchase must satisfy exactly.\n\n12. BUDGET-LINE SHIFTS AND ROTATIONS\nIf income rises while both prices remain fixed, the budget line shifts outward in PARALLEL; its slope does not change.\nIf the price of X falls while income and the price of Y stay fixed, the X-intercept moves outward while the Y-intercept remains fixed: the line ROTATES.\nIf both prices and income double, the set of affordable bundles is unchanged.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nIncome ₦1,200; X ₦200; Y ₦300.\nIntercepts: X = 6, Y = 4.\nIncome rises to ₦1,800, prices unchanged.\nNew intercepts: X = 9, Y = 6. This is a parallel outward shift.\n\n13. INDIFFERENCE CURVES — WHY THEY DO NOT CROSS\nSuppose two curves intersect at the same bundle A. Each curve would assign a different satisfaction ranking to other bundles while treating both as indifferent to A. That conflicts with consistent (transitive) preferences. Under standard assumptions, indifference curves therefore cannot intersect.\n\nConvexity reflects a preference for balanced combinations: as a person gets more X and less Y, they are usually willing to sacrifice progressively less Y for an additional X.\n\n14. NORMAL, INFERIOR AND GIFFEN GOODS\nFor a normal good, higher income increases demand. For an inferior good, higher income decreases demand, other things equal.\nA Giffen good is a rare special case of an inferior good where a price rise increases quantity demanded because the negative income effect is unusually strong and outweighs the substitution effect.\n\nNot all inferior goods are Giffen goods. A cheap staple can be inferior without displaying Giffen behaviour.\n\n15. THE LAW OF EQUI-MARGINAL UTILITY\nThe consumer allocates spending across uses so that the last unit of money spent on each gives equal marginal utility, under standard interior conditions. This is also called the principle of equi-marginal returns in consumer choice.\n\nIt does NOT say the total utility of all goods must be equal, or that the consumer must buy equal quantities of each good.\n\n16. EXAM DIAGNOSTIC QUESTIONS\n• “Additional satisfaction from one extra unit” → marginal utility.\n• “All combinations giving equal satisfaction” → indifference curve.\n• “Maximum affordable combinations” → budget line.\n• “The price of one good changes” → budget-line rotation.\n• “Income changes, prices constant” → parallel budget-line shift.\n• “The consumer is willing to pay more than market price” → consumer surplus.\n\nEXTRA REVISION WITH EXPLANATIONS\n1. TU increases from 42 to 48 when quantity increases from 3 to 4. MU = 6.\n2. A consumer's income doubles, and both prices double. Does the budget line change? No; purchasing opportunities are unchanged.\n3. Why can TU rise while MU falls? Each additional unit still adds positive satisfaction, but less than the previous unit.\n4. Is every inferior good a Giffen good? No. Giffen behaviour requires the income effect to outweigh the substitution effect.\n5. At an interior ordinal optimum, what equals the price ratio? Marginal rate of substitution.\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. TU rises from 22 to 30 as quantity rises from 2 to 3. MU? 8 utils.\n2. What happens to TU while MU is positive? TU increases.\n3. What does one indifference curve represent? Bundles giving equal satisfaction.\n4. Income ₦1,200, X price ₦200. Maximum X if no Y is bought? 6.\n5. What condition describes an interior cardinal optimum? MUx/Px = MUy/Py.\n6. Willingness to pay ₦5,000, actual price ₦3,500. Consumer surplus? ₦1,500."}}];
